WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990
- When you take on an
engagement as a lawyer,

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:03.740
the worst thing that you
think that can happen

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.380
is you'll lose the case.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.450
You don't think you're gonna
get disbarred as a result

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:10.030
and you certainly don't think
that you're going to jail,

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:13.170
especially when you're
suing a private company.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.200
But some people have accused Chevron,

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.720
the oil company, of doing just that,

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.300
putting lawyer Steven Donziger in jail

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.420
for his lawsuit against them.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.970
But is that what really happened

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.560
and is that what happened
in this particular case?

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.630
Well, once upon a time,

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.380
lawyer Steven Donziger famously
won a case against Chevron

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.670
for polluting the Amazonian
rainforest in the Ecuador.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.130
He won billions of dollars,

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.860
but Donziger has been
disbarred as under house arrest

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.150
and the plaintiffs in that
case have never been paid.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.390
And now the eyes of the legal
world are on New York City

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.140
where Donziger's contempt of court trial

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.040
has captured the attention of celebrities,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.090
legal commentators and social media.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.673
So what is the Steven
Donziger case all about?

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:55.953
Hey, Legal Eagles,

00:00:55.953 --> 00:00:58.300
it's time to think like a
lawyer under house arrest

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.400
because that's where
Steven Donziger has been

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.950
while he awaits the outcome
of a contempt of court trial

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.690
that stems from a case he
started way back in 1993.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.130
So today we're going to analyze

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.400
the Chevron vs Steve Donziger ordeal.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.290
How do we get here?

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.830
Who bribed whom and who is the real threat

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.890
to the rule of law,

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:18.150
former attorney Steve
Donziger or Chevron Oil?

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.600
But to understand what's
happening right now in New York,

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.220
we have to understand
what some people call

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.920
the Amazon Chernobyl.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.510
The Donziger saga involves
two different legal disputes.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.510
First, there was the case
where indigenous people

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:31.180
from Ecuador sued Chevron
for destroying parts

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.620
of the Amazon rainforest.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.460
Steve Donziger was their lawyer.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.930
And although the plaintiff's won,

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.800
they have been unable to
collect the $9.5 billion

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.120
for legal issues that we'll
talk about in a second.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.190
The second dispute is Chevron civil case

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.790
against Donziger himself,

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.460
where the company has accused him of fraud

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.550
and bribery in violation of
American racketeering laws.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.270
Donziger lost this lawsuit
and then failed to comply

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.730
with multiple court orders

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.750
that led to contempt of court charges

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.580
and this is why Donziger
is currently on trial.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.350
But the story of this
goes way back to the 1960s

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.010
when Texaco teamed up
with a military junta

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.660
that it seized power in
Ecuador to drill for oil

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:07.650
in the Amazon.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.900
These tribes were hunter gatherers

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.690
who didn't even have a word for oil,

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.800
but it's alleged that
Texaco's activities produced

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.560
16 billion gallons of toxic runoff,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.010
a lot of which wound up in
unregulated shallow pits,

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.670
but most of the toxic sludge
was simply dumped into rivers.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:22.830
To facilitate the drilling,

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.520
Texaco created a company
town called Lago Agrio,

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.500
which became a typical oil boom town

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.000
with all the components needed
for drilling infrastructure,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.570
concrete walls, holding
tanks, massive drills

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:34.400
and miles of pipelines.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.560
But in 1992,

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.520
Texaco ended its Ecuador operation

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.040
leaving behind this infrastructure

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.880
plus hundreds of open oil pits,

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.760
thousands of waste pits near
drinking and fishing water

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.230
and untold amounts of
contaminated wastewater.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.260
How bad was the situation in Ecuador?

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.820
Well, even now more than 50 years

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.350
after Texaco started drilling,

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.050
the people who live there
can't get clean water

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.610
from the river so they collect rainwater

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.210
or walk six miles to
fill plastic water jugs

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.140
from a water truck provided
by a Spanish oil company.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.900
This is why the situation for local people

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.270
has been called the Amazon Chernobyl

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:05.800
and it's also why in 1993,

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.470
Donziger and a small team of lawyers

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.500
filed a lawsuit on behalf
of the people of Lago Agrio.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.770
They filed the case in New York

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.380
where Texaco was headquartered.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.720
The company whose assets were
eventually acquired by Chevron

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.760
sought to remove the
litigation to Ecuador,

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.150
meaning take the litigation
from the United States

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.870
and put it in Ecuador.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.470
Strategically, the company assumed

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.600
that they would have a better shot

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.150
at winning the case in Ecuador,

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.120
where they still had political clout.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.900
This complicated jurisdictional battle

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.900
took 10 years to resolve.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.380
Chevron won and the case
was removed to Ecuador

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:37.760
and Chevron has always maintained

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.110
that Ecuadorian contamination

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.950
is the responsibility of Texaco,

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.000
even though Chevron inherited
the company's assets

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.800
around the world and runs
Texaco as a subsidiary.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.110
And it also puts a fair amount
of blame on Petroecuador,

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.050
the state run petrochemical company.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:50.980
Not surprisingly though,

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.960
even after the case
was removed to Ecuador,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.550
the case still took years to complete

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.300
as the parties to the suit
and independent groups

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.500
completed over 60,000 lab
tests on soil and water.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.240
The results quote overwhelmingly
showed contamination

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.810
traceable to Texaco,

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.520
but the testing process was contentious

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.970
and both sides crossed ethical lines.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.140
So we'll start with
Chevron's dirty dealing.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.270
It's alleged that Chevron
concocted a scheme

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:14.950
where it sent soil and water samples

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.400
to a friendly lab for favorable results.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.180
We know about this because Diego Borja,

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.280
a Chevron contractor
who had been the Chevron

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.820
worked with his uncle and wife

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.380
to create four front companies,

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.940
which posed as independent labs.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.100
There are recordings of
Borja discussing his ability

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.040
to ruse enough evidence

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.770
to swing the case to the
plaintiffs if need be.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.490
He said that Chevron understood
exactly what he was implying

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:36.630
and that the company promised

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.750
that he would be taken care of.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.280
Crime does pay, Borja had
laughed in one of these calls.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.120
Chevron eventually helped
Borja and his family

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.180
move to the U.S.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.230
Chevron paid Borja's legal fees,

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:49.800
helping him to become a
naturalized American citizen.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.170
The company also paid his
rent and gave his family

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:53.530
a monthly stipend.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:56.030
Chevron paid Borja over $2 million

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.010
and those are just the payments

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.200
the plaintiffs were able to trace.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.430
This raises a question,

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.060
is it legal to pay a non-expert
witness for testimony?

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.170
And the answer to that is actually no.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.060
Fact witnesses generally may be reimbursed

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.560
for expenses incurred in time

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:10.100
lost in connection with litigation,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.400
but they can't be paid
to testify or not testify

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.520
for the substance of their testimony.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.040
Chevron has declined to
comment on Borja's allegations

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.020
beyond stating that they are

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.160
protecting him from retaliation.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.090
An American judge denied further discovery

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.010
into the nature of the
Borja Chevron arrangement.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.300
But let's talk about the allegations

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.880
against Donziger himself.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.410
Chevron has accused
Donziger of dirty dealing

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.720
and in a diary entry later
obtained by a U.S. court,

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.080
Donziger admits that
he had a secret contact

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.610
with the court appointed expert

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.580
who was supposed to be independent.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:40.880
Donziger described the situation

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.870
as a quote bargain with the devil.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.600
Quote we can't win with the
devil because they, Chevron,

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.210
can always pay more.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:48.740
There's also evidence that Donziger paid

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.130
for the expert report and
may have written parts of it

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.800
although American lawyers
don't write expert reports.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.810
In our legal system,
lawyers do pay experts

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.550
and exert some influence
how the reports are written.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.290
In the Ecuadorian case, the expert report

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:02.600
was written by a Colorado
company called Stratus.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.160
Many experts say that the expert report

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.760
scientific analysis was sound.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.170
However, according to a
report in Rolling Stone,

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:09.310
Chevron played hardball

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:10.880
with Stratus, pressuring
insurance companies

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:12.700
to cancel its coverage with Stratus

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.060
and leaning on its clients

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:15.820
to cancel contracts with the company.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.900
Eventually Stratus faced
bankruptcy in a move

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:19.220
that may or may not be related

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.010
its executive signed an
agreement with Chevron

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:24.140
explicitly disavowing its
work in Ecuador as fraudulent.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.480
But to get back to the verdict
in the Ecuadorian case,

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.490
this issue with the Stratus
report may be nothing more

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:30.620
than a distraction from
the bigger picture.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.650
The Ecuadorian court
granted Chevron's motion

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.190
to exclude the expert
report from consideration.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:36.023
In the end,

00:06:36.023 --> 00:06:37.770
the Ecuadorian court
found for the plaintiffs

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.000
without relying on the
controversial report.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.210
And this nine year legal saga generated

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:46.610
a 220,000 page record with
over 100 expert reports,

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:48.270
testimony from dozens of witnesses,

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.830
scientific data from 54
court supervised inspections

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.630
and independent health evaluations.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:54.463
And in the end,

00:06:54.463 --> 00:06:55.390
the court found the Texaco Chevron

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.010
had dumped billions of gallons of water

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:58.900
containing toxic chemicals in waterways

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.210
and on the jungle floor,

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.570
used waste pits that contaminated the soil

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:05.530
and groundwater, never cleaned
up the frequent oil spills,

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.280
maintain a policy of destroying documents

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.020
related to oil spills
and burning natural gas

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.520
that contaminated the air.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.150
On February 14th, 2011,

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.100
a judge entered a judgment against Chevron

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.810
for over $19 billion,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.660
which was later reduced to $9.5 billion.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:23.600
It was upheld by Ecuadorian
appeals courts in 2012 and 2013.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.030
And this was pretty
much the last good thing

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.520
that happened for the
Lago Agrio plaintiffs.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.090
So that takes us to the American case

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.290
of Chevron versus Donziger.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.730
After Chevron lost the Ecuadorian case,

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.320
it dropped a bombshell that would change

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:38.070
the trajectory of this whole entire saga.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:41.310
They alleged the Donziger
bribed an Ecuadorian judge.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:42.143
After the trial,

00:07:42.143 --> 00:07:44.050
former Ecuadorean judge Alberto Guerra

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:45.950
came forward with a
story that the legal team

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:48.040
for the Ecuadorians offered him a bribe

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.700
to ghost write the
judgment against Chevron.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.300
Guerra said that he asked
Chevron for a bribe first

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.170
and they turned him down so
he then went to Donziger.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:59.610
He says he saw bribes on behalf
of judge Nicholas Zambrano,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.660
the judge who actually
issued the final judgment

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:02.493
in the case.

00:08:02.493 --> 00:08:03.570
And according to Guerra,

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.577
he and Zambrano shared
$500,000 in kickbacks

00:08:06.577 --> 00:08:08.460
paid by Donziger.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.070
This is the testimony that Chevron used

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.980
to sue Donziger under U.S.
anti-racketeering laws,

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:16.390
which takes us to a rare case of RICO.

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:18.410
The organized crime control act of 1970,

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.410
also known as the Racketeer Influenced

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.650
and Corrupt Organizations act or RICO

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:23.720
was originally enacted

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.810
to help the government
stop organized crime.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:27.790
RICO generally has two different flavors,

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:29.080
civil and criminal.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.240
The government can prosecute
a person who operates

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.710
or requires an enterprise
through a pattern

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.880
of racketeering activity.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.790
RICO's criminal penalties
include imprisonment

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:40.570
up to 20 years and even life
depending on the activity.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:42.520
And when Congress passed RICO,

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.120
their expectation was that the
vast majority of RICO claims

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.030
would be brought by federal prosecutors.

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:48.890
However, the law also
had a civil component

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.480
that allows private persons and entities

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.450
to file RICO lawsuits.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.270
RICO civil remedies
provision section 1964C

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:57.520
states quote any person
injured in his business

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.070
or property by reason of a
violation of section 1962

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.010
of his chapter may sue therefore

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:03.470
in any appropriate U.S. district court

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:06.260
and shall recover threefold
the damages he sustains

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:07.490
and the cost of the suit,

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:10.010
including reasonable attorney's fees.

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:13.190
Chevron as a private party
and person uses section

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:14.740
to argue that the Ecuadorian verdict

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:16.340
was obtained fraudulently.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:18.670
And while the Ecuadorian
verdict was upheld on appeal

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.800
in Ecuador, Chevron doesn't
need an Ecuadorian court.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:23.330
All it needed to do was cast out

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:24.960
on the original legal proceedings,

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.530
which would of course help
them to avoid paying a judgment

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:28.670
in the United States.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:30.490
So let's talk about
Guerra's testimony here.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:32.590
In 2011, Chevron sued Donziger

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:33.960
and the plaintiffs alleging fraud

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.340
and using Guerra's
testimony to prove its case.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.173
During the litigation,

00:09:37.173 --> 00:09:38.640
Guerra admitted that when he was a judge,

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.970
he accepted bribes and fixed cases.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:43.690
He also admitted that Chevron
relocated him and his family

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:46.000
to the U.S. and compensated
him in much the same way

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.310
that it paid Borja.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:49.260
However, Chevron says
Guerra legitimately feared

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.560
for his life and facilitated
a move to the United States

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:52.393
to protect him.

00:09:52.393 --> 00:09:53.820
Erwin Chemerinsky, basically the guy

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.050
that everyone learns constitutional
law from in law school

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.040
and also the Dean

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.900
of the University of California
Irvine School of Law,

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.680
signed a declaration
criticizing the Guerra evidence

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:05.920
quote if a party or its
counsel were permitted

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.240
to pay a testifying witness
for physical evidence

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.940
beyond the reasonable
value of that evidence

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:11.850
and to pay the witness
a salary in exchange

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.260
for an agreement to testify,

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:14.780
there'll be little left to the rule

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.840
against compensating fact witnesses.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:18.880
That is precisely what has occurred here.

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:21.250
Not withstanding, U.S.
district judge Lewin Kaplan

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:23.420
found clear and convincing
evidence that Donziger

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:24.840
and the plaintiffs committed fraud,

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.090
bribery and extortion to
obtain judgment over Chevron,

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:30.020
quote Guerra on many occasions

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.270
has acted deceitfully and broken the law,

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:33.610
but that does not necessarily mean

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:35.830
that it should be disregarded wholesale.

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:37.570
Judge Kaplan also issued a new junction

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:38.770
barring the Ecuadorian plaintiffs

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:41.160
from enforcing the judgment
in American courts.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.180
Now Chevron leveraged the Kaplan decision

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:44.830
in the Guerra evidence internationally,

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:47.020
convincing an arbitration
tribunal in the Hague

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:49.655
that the Ecuadorian judgment
violin Chevron's rights.

00:10:49.655 --> 00:10:51.990
Interestingly, at the arbitration
in front of the Hague,

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.130
Guerra recanted his testimony again

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:56.730
and claimed that he lied about
the whole kickback scheme.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:57.860
He claims he approached Chevron

00:10:57.860 --> 00:10:59.550
with an offer to manufacture testimony

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:01.380
and to increase his bargaining position.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:04.370
He told Chevron plaintiffs
offered him a bribe

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:06.550
and Guerra testified that
Chevron promised to pay him

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:07.630
for making up a story.

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:08.550
During his testimony,

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:10.010
Guerra admitted that he has no evidence

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:12.110
of the alleged bribes and forensic experts

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:14.030
from both parties agree
that the draft judgment

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:15.810
was never found on either Steve Donziger's

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.290
or Guerra's computer, but
similar to the American courts,

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:20.420
the Hague tribunal rule
that no court should enforce

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:21.650
the judgment because parts of it

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:23.250
were corruptly ghost written

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.340
in return for the promise of a bribe.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:27.180
Now, after judge Kaplan's ruling,

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:29.240
Donziger appealed to the
U.S. Court of Appeals

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:30.160
for the second circuit,

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:31.700
which is the Court of Appeals

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.730
is one step below the Supreme Court.

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:36.100
Donziger argued that Judge
Kaplan's expansive reading

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.690
of the RICO statute was
not supported by case law.

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:41.790
The second circuit disagreed
finding that section 1964

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.620
was meant to allow private
citizens to be prosecutors

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.380
or attorneys general in
this private right of action

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:49.600
quote section 1964 subsection A

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:52.040
gives federal courts
jurisdiction to hear RICO claims

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:53.590
and sets out general remedies

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:54.800
including injunctive relief.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:56.130
Subsection B makes it clear

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:58.970
that the court on the application
of the attorney general

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:01.100
has authority to grant
temporary injunctive relief

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:04.270
even before its final
adjudication and subsection C

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:06.470
provides a private right
of action for any person

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.370
injured in his business or property

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:11.730
by reasons in violation of section 1962.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:13.880
The second circuit held that
there is no legal principle

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.660
that stops the U.S. court
from granting a company relief

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.200
from a foreign verdict.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.280
And Judge Kaplan's orders
certainly aided Chevron's attempts

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:22.350
to avoid judgment.

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:24.500
And not only did this injunction bar

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.560
in a U.S. court from
enforcing the judgment,

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:28.210
it also set up a constructive trust

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.450
that would hold any proceeds
that plaintiffs obtained

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.690
anywhere else in the world.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:33.520
Now, some have criticized the strategy

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.150
and the second circuit's decision

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:37.830
as giving private actors like corporations

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.520
and other way to go after people
who criticize their actions

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.200
in the context of free speech.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.620
This is known as generally a SLAPP suit.

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:45.760
A SLAPP is an acronym

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:48.080
for a strategic lawsuit
against public participation.

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.900
And it's interesting because
in this particular case,

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:54.990
what Chevron argued successfully
was that getting a lawsuit

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:58.190
or at least extorting,
allegedly extorting a lawsuit

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:03.190
in a foreign jurisdiction was
a violation of RICO statute.

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:06.020
Even if a verdict is
obtained in bad faith,

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:09.120
it's extremely unusual
to claim that a lawsuit

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:10.940
or the legal system in general

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:13.720
is an arm of a RICO enterprise,

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:15.420
but that more or less takes
us to the contempt trial,

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:16.830
which is why Steven Donziger

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:19.240
is under house arrest to this day.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:21.930
Based on the allegations of
impropriety against Donziger

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:22.960
or I guess at this point

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:25.850
the findings of impropriety
against Donziger,

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:27.960
Chevron sued Donziger personally,

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.900
and the court ordered
Donziger to pay Chevron money

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.510
that he had earned by selling shares

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:34.190
in the Ecuadorian judgment.

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:38.750
Eventually Kaplan ordered
Donziger to pay Chevron $813,000.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:40.230
This is the order that has led

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:42.010
to Donziger's current trial.

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:43.400
Chevron filed motions with the court,

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.370
alleging that Donziger was hiding assets

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:46.780
in order to avoid paying up.

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:48.920
And the court ordered Donziger
to produce his cell phone,

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.220
computer and other documents

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.100
so that the court could review
evidence about his assets.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:54.530
Donziger refused to comply

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:55.880
with the court's discovery orders.

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:58.270
He argued that the materials
would reveal decades

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:00.210
of communications he had with his clients,

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:02.140
which arguably would be protected

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:03.620
by attorney client privilege.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:05.000
And while that might be true,

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:06.580
even if this information would reveal

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:08.140
some protected communications,

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.460
courts deal with this kind
of sensitive information

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:11.320
all the time.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.410
Judges often review
the material in camera,

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:16.030
which means only the court
is looking at these documents

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:17.980
and they often allow redactions

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.180
and they can issue orders
preventing the other side

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.090
from seeing protected information,

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:24.570
but by refusing to comply
with the court's orders,

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.200
Donziger found himself
in contempt of court.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:29.600
And here's where things
went sideways again.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:32.360
The judge, Judge Kaplan
appointed a special prosecutor,

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:34.820
Rita Glavin, a former DOJ attorney now

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:37.250
in private practice to
handle the contempt case.

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:40.230
Glavin's role was to prosecute
on behalf of the court.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.560
The Manhattan AUSA twice told the judge

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:45.250
that it was overtaxed and
didn't have any lawyers to spare

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:46.600
in this contempt trial.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:49.280
Kaplan went ahead with the
prosecution using Glavin

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:50.370
and something else that's unusual,

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:52.130
the maximum penalty for
misdemeanor contempt

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:53.125
to federal court

00:14:53.125 --> 00:14:54.940
in this situation is about
six months in prison.

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:56.320
However, the judge ordered Donziger

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:58.700
to be held under home confinement

00:14:58.700 --> 00:14:59.960
because he's allegedly a flight risk

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:01.600
due to his interest in Ecuador.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:05.430
Donziger has been under house
arrest for more than 600 days,

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:06.620
more than four times

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:08.800
the length of time that he
could be confined in prison

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.320
if he was convicted of the offense.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.980
Donziger supporters, which
include Nobel laureates

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.780
and international law organizations

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:16.080
said that this is unprecedented

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.880
for a federal misdemeanor case.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.760
They and Donziger also argued

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.790
that given the punitive
nature of this prosecution,

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:23.980
he's entitled to a trial by jury.

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:26.260
The court has disagreed so Donziger's fate

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:27.960
will be decided by a judge.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.780
Thus Donziger appears to
be in a Catch 22 situation.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:32.970
He's been confined for more than the time

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:33.970
that he could be sent to jail

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:36.250
and is on the hook for $800,000.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:37.820
Meanwhile, the facts that
have been established

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:40.570
in the American federal courts
say that he bribed a judge

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:41.403
and the Supreme Court

00:15:41.403 --> 00:15:43.630
has denied Donziger's
petition for certiorari.

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:45.940
So this means that the RICO case is over.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:47.330
So the criminal contempt case

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:50.700
is an offshoot of Chevron
civil action against Donziger.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:51.533
In that case,

00:15:51.533 --> 00:15:53.240
judge Kaplan ruled that Donziger obtained

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:56.550
the $8.6 billion judgment
in Ecuador against Chevron

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.550
through fraudulent and corrupt means.

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:00.710
Judge Kaplan's judgment enjoying Donziger

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:02.410
from enforcing the Ecuadorian judgment

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:04.900
in the United States were
attempting to profit from it.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:07.980
Donziger appealed his
case to the Supreme Court,

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:09.760
which did not grant certiorari,

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:12.380
which means the Supreme Court
did not take up the case.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.120
And that meant that Chevron won the case

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:17.140
and could move on to enforce
the judgment of the court.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.770
And when a plaintiff wins a judgment,

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:21.090
the next step usually includes
determining what assets

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.510
the defendant has to satisfy
the monetary judgment.

00:16:23.510 --> 00:16:24.610
Against that backdrop,

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:25.990
Judge Kaplan ordered Donziger

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:29.010
to transfer to Chevron
all property that he had,

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:30.550
or might later obtain

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:33.160
that could be traced to
the Ecuadorian judgment.

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:36.140
And in 2018, Chevron initiated
post-judgment discovery

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:37.490
to identify assets available

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:40.030
to enforce a judgment against Donziger.

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:42.170
So discovery can come at
the beginning of a case

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:44.160
before the case is over
and it can also come

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:46.630
after for the case when
you have to figure out

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:48.030
how you're going to get the money

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:50.680
from the person that you
just won the money against.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:52.300
And the court also ordered discovery

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:54.070
to assess Donziger's compliance

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:56.110
with the judgment's injunctive provisions.

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:58.040
Donziger resisted this discovery

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.810
refusing to provide the
requested information

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:01.560
and refusing to transfer property.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:03.490
He sent a letter to judge Kaplan stating

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:05.320
that he would prefer to be
found in contempt of court

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:07.020
and then challenged the contempt findings

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:08.480
in court proceedings.

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.190
The judge said Donziger should turn over

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:11.993
his electronic devices
to a forensic expert,

00:17:11.993 --> 00:17:14.020
so they could be imaged and examined

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:15.690
for responsive documents.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.120
Donziger refused to do this

00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:19.940
and so he was found in contempt of court

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:21.360
and in the latest proceedings,

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:23.690
Judge Preska clearly expects Donziger

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:26.730
to attack her impartiality
as he's done in the past

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.430
and as he's done with Judge
Kaplan and in late July,

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:31.420
Judge Preska finally issued her rule

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:33.560
and she found Donziger
guilty of six counts

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:34.770
of contempt of court.

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:38.550
The 245 page ruling is
bound to be controversial.

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:40.040
Judge Preska agreed with Donziger

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.680
that he may be the only
lawyer ever prosecuted

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:44.180
by a private special counsel

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:45.480
in the Southern district of New York.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:47.790
The judge said that the
uniqueness of the circumstances

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:50.700
didn't render the prosecution
inappropriate or untoward.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.630
Judge Preska's ruling
said that Donziger refused

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:53.860
to comply with the court orders

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:56.440
and that was an attack
on the rule of law quote

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:58.460
at stake here is the fundamental principle

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:00.110
that a party to illegal action

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:02.920
must abide by court orders
or risk criminal sanctions,

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:04.450
no matter how fervently he believes

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:06.130
in the righteousness of his cause,

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:08.950
or how much he detests his adversary.

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:10.960
The judge did not
consider the wider context

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.480
of the Chevron saga except to point out

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:14.950
that she believed Donziger
was sincere in his beliefs

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:17.810
that he's being persecuted
and so are his clients.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.310
As the judge pointed out, quote,

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:21.280
this case, however, is wholly unconcerned

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:23.140
with the debate regarding
any responsibility

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:25.230
Chevron might bear for that pollution.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:26.900
The only question before this court

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.180
was the very specific
question of whether Donziger

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:32.300
had committed criminal contempt
in violation of 18 USC 401.

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:34.420
To secure a criminal
conviction for contempt

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:35.440
of a court order,

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:37.060
prosecutors have to prove three elements.

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:38.830
One that the order was issued.

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:40.730
Two, that the defendant's disobedience

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:43.220
were disregard of that order and three,

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:44.760
the defendant's knowledge or willfulness

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:46.100
in disobeying that order.

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.010
The judge ruled that the
prosecutor had proved

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:50.030
beyond a reasonable doubt
that Donziger received notice

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:52.200
of the court orders that
he ignored the court orders

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:53.870
and that he did so willfully.

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:55.310
Donziger on the other hand argued

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:57.070
that his acts were ethically grounded

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:59.960
in principled objections that
lawyers are permitted to make

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:01.530
when they object to an abuse of power.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:03.130
He cited the case of In re McConnell,

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:05.850
which reversed a contempt
conviction of a lawyer

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:08.210
who insisted on pursuing a forbidden line

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:09.530
of questioning at trial.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:11.920
However, Judge Preska said that
McConnell didn't apply here

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:13.810
since the contempt charges in that case

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:16.180
were under a different section of 401,

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:17.480
which prohibits in court acts

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:19.570
that quote obstruct the
administration of justice.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.010
Rather than the third
section of section 401,

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:24.430
which is the statute that
Donziger was charged under.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:27.630
Section 4013 prohibits disobedience

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:29.140
of lawful court orders.

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.290
The judge also took issue
with Donziger's assumption

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:34.090
that the McConnell case allows
lawyers to defy court orders

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.830
so long as their decisions
were quote ethically grounded.

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:38.720
Quote criminal contempt jurisprudence

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:41.600
has no safe harbor for
conscientious objectors.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:43.130
Indeed, the law is clear that

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:46.320
a party may not challenge a
court's order by violating it.

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:49.550
Instead, he must move to
vacate or modify the order

00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:51.940
or seek relief from the Court of Appeals.

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:53.000
Since this was not a trial

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.760
about Chevron's
responsibility for pollution,

